% ============================================================================
% solar-eclipse-type — three named solar eclipse types and what each
% actually is (adj-facts-stdlib, ASTRONOMY).
% ============================================================================
% A solar eclipse happens when the Moon passes between the Sun and Earth,
% but not every solar eclipse looks the same: whether the Sun's face is
% completely blocked, only partly blocked, or blocked with a bright ring
% left showing depends on how exactly the three bodies line up and how far
% the Moon is from Earth at the time. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? solar_eclipse_type(total_solar_eclipse, $D)
%       % completely_blocking_the_face_of_the_sun
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground
% relation `solar_eclipse_type(type, description)` carrying the source
% citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the
% engine returns the description WITH its source, on the CPU, with zero
% model calls, and abstains on an eclipse type that is not one of these
% three. The query also runs BACKWARD — bind a description and recall the
% eclipse type it names:
%
%     ? solar_eclipse_type($T, moon_at_or_near_its_farthest_point_from_earth)
%       % annular_solar_eclipse
%
% The three eclipse types, as a little truth table:
%
%     type                        description                                       quote (verbatim)
%     ---------------------------  -------------------------------------------------  --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
%     total_solar_eclipse           completely_blocking_the_face_of_the_sun            "A total solar eclipse happens when the Moon passes between the Sun and Earth, completely blocking the face of the Sun."
%     annular_solar_eclipse         moon_at_or_near_its_farthest_point_from_earth      "An annular solar eclipse happens when the Moon passes between the Sun and Earth, but when it is at or near its farthest point from Earth."
%     partial_solar_eclipse         sun_moon_and_earth_not_perfectly_lined_up          "A partial solar eclipse happens when the Moon passes between the Sun and Earth but the Sun, Moon, and Earth are not perfectly lined up."
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from NASA's "Types of Solar Eclipses" page,
% which defines each of these three eclipse types in its own single
% sentence. The original authoring note said the quotes were
% "WebFetch-verified before writing (twice)"; the raw-page measurement
% below supersedes it. `trust authoritative` — NASA is a primary .gov
% source, the same tier the sibling `moon-phases.adj` (the other library
% in this directory) already uses for its NASA citation. An eclipse type
% that is NOT one of these three — "hybrid_solar_eclipse", a real eclipse
% type the same page also names, but whose own explanation takes TWO
% sentences (how Earth's curved surface lets an eclipse shift between
% annular and total) rather than one clean quotable sentence like the
% three tabled here — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall ABSTAINS on
% it rather than inventing a single-sentence description the source never
% gave.
%
% EACH ROW CARRIES ITS OWN SENTENCE (ADJ-TABLES RS-5e, #14986). The
% envelope used to be the total_solar_eclipse span, so an annular or
% partial answer was warranted primarily by a sentence about a TOTAL
% eclipse, and those two sentences reached no answer at all. Each row now
% carries the sentence defining its own type, and the envelope is the
% page's sentence defining a solar eclipse as such, which names none of
% the three types:
%
%     "A solar eclipse happens when the Moon passes between the Sun and
%      Earth, casting a shadow on Earth that either fully or partially
%      blocks the Sun’s light in some areas."
%
% The quoted envelope above carries the page's own U+2019 apostrophe in
% “Sun’s”. Counted on the fetched page: that spelling of the ENVELOPE
% SENTENCE occurs once, and the same sentence with an ASCII apostrophe
% occurs zero times. (The prose of this header uses ASCII apostrophes
% throughout; the count is about the quoted span, not about the file.)
%
% Measured 2026-09-16 UTC by this table's own fetch: the page answers 200
% in 276381 bytes, and a nonsense path on the same host answers 404 with a
% full styled 223301-byte error page rather than a small stub -- so the
% control here is that the spans are absent from a LARGE page, not that
% the page is empty. Tags removed without a space and only ASCII
% whitespace collapsed: each row's span and the envelope each occur
% exactly once, inside one `<p>`, scripts set aside and over the whole
% file, with no `<head>` copy. Every row's page is the envelope's, so no
% row restates a `locator` line and no row repeats the `trust` tier.
%
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table solar_eclipse_type {
    columns type, description

    row (total_solar_eclipse, completely_blocking_the_face_of_the_sun) {
        source "A total solar eclipse happens when the Moon passes between the Sun and Earth, completely blocking the face of the Sun."
    }
    row (annular_solar_eclipse, moon_at_or_near_its_farthest_point_from_earth) {
        source "An annular solar eclipse happens when the Moon passes between the Sun and Earth, but when it is at or near its farthest point from Earth."
    }
    row (partial_solar_eclipse, sun_moon_and_earth_not_perfectly_lined_up) {
        source "A partial solar eclipse happens when the Moon passes between the Sun and Earth but the Sun, Moon, and Earth are not perfectly lined up."
    }

    source "A solar eclipse happens when the Moon passes between the Sun and Earth, casting a shadow on Earth that either fully or partially blocks the Sun’s light in some areas."
    locator "https://science.nasa.gov/eclipses/types/"
    trust authoritative
}
